WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.637
Tree growth, wood anatomy and tree-ring structure.

00:00:04.092 --> 00:00:08.020
This explanation supports a ninety-minute classroom lesson.

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:13.921
Distinguish organs, tissues and cells, and pause the video to complete the observation

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:15.792
exercises.

00:00:16.595 --> 00:00:18.517
Locate wood production.

00:00:18.979 --> 00:00:23.380
Explain the vascular cambium and secondary growth in a diagram.

00:00:23.834 --> 00:00:26.005
Read boundaries from anatomy.

00:00:26.466 --> 00:00:31.610
Compare softwood, ring-porous and diffuse-porous wood using visible features.

00:00:32.071 --> 00:00:34.012
Interpret a ring count.

00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:39.380
Consider sampling height, missing pith, false rings and absent rings.

00:00:39.834 --> 00:00:45.051
Use visible features to explain the material rather than only memorizing terms.

00:00:45.505 --> 00:00:50.548
Record observations separately from the interpretations you propose.

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:53.280
Year of ring formation.

00:00:53.735 --> 00:00:57.814
Link this to felling or construction using additional evidence.

00:00:58.274 --> 00:01:00.057
Annual increments.

00:01:00.511 --> 00:01:03.649
Check whether a growth ring represents one year.

00:01:04.103 --> 00:01:05.761
Question for today.

00:01:06.222 --> 00:01:09.704
Will simply counting dark lines give a correct date?.

00:01:10.011 --> 00:01:14.985
A calendar date for a ring does not necessarily date germination or felling with the same

00:01:14.998 --> 00:01:16.090
precision.

00:01:16.551 --> 00:01:19.784
Consider what part of the tree survives.

00:01:20.587 --> 00:01:24.022
Leaves: photosynthesis and gas exchange.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.925
Stem: support, transport and storage.

00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:31.267
Roots: water and nutrient uptake.

00:01:31.721 --> 00:01:36.267
Photosynthesis, water uptake and stem transport are connected.

00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:42.492
Interpret stem growth as part of a living organism rather than an isolated clock.

00:01:43.295 --> 00:01:47.296
Primary growth Apical meristems extend shoots and roots.

00:01:47.758 --> 00:01:51.672
Secondary growth Cambial activity adds wood and thickness.

00:01:52.126 --> 00:01:57.185
Growth in length and growth in thickness involve different meristems and directions.

00:01:57.639 --> 00:02:01.504
Keep their positions distinct when reading the diagram.

00:02:02.307 --> 00:02:04.650
Inward: secondary xylem.

00:02:05.112 --> 00:02:07.612
Outward: secondary phloem.

00:02:08.072 --> 00:02:11.158
The cambium lies between xylem and phloem.

00:02:11.612 --> 00:02:15.421
The cambium produces xylem inward and phloem outward.

00:02:15.875 --> 00:02:21.572
Locate these tissues in the schematic before interpreting boundaries in real material.

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:23.988
Tissue or cell.

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:27.147
Typical state and function at maturity.

00:02:27.601 --> 00:02:30.081
Tracheids / vessel elements.

00:02:30.535 --> 00:02:34.469
Usually dead; involved in water conduction and support.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:36.765
Sapwood parenchyma.

00:02:37.219 --> 00:02:40.719
Includes living cells for storage and metabolism.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.765
Cambial cells.

00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:46.897
Living meristem supplying xylem and phloem cells.

00:02:47.351 --> 00:02:51.311
Wood contains cell types with different functions and lifetimes.

00:02:51.772 --> 00:02:56.364
Distinguish mature conducting cells from living parenchyma rather than treating all wood

00:02:56.377 --> 00:02:57.864
alike.

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:02.556
Sapwood Outer wood involved in water transport and storage.

00:03:03.011 --> 00:03:07.992
Heartwood Inner, transformed wood, usually no longer conducting water.

00:03:08.446 --> 00:03:11.873
A colour change is not itself a growth-ring boundary.

00:03:12.327 --> 00:03:16.768
The sapwood-heartwood boundary is different from an annual growth boundary.

00:03:17.222 --> 00:03:21.340
Colour alone does not establish the year a tree was felled.
